{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Provide a logical volume mounted read-write at /srv/data.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A 1.00 GiB LVM logical volume was created, formatted as ext4, and directly observed mounted read-write at /srv/data. After the mandatory restart, the 2.00 GiB LV was again active and mounted at the same path.","evidence_ids":["cmd-02f64ca6f129430c","cmd-133e5533403f4c2c","cmd-2192380bdf204711"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Grow the logical volume to at least 2 GB while it remains mounted and preserve its contents.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The volume was observed mounted immediately before growth at 1.00 GiB, remained mounted through the operation, and was observed afterward at exactly 2,147,483,648 bytes with its ext4 filesystem expanded online. The sentinel file retained the identical SHA-256 hash before and after growth and remained intact after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2b402f6770ff43d4","cmd-2192380bdf204711"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ubuntu 16.04.7 LTS and kernel 4.4.0-210-generic match before and after execution. The installed legacy LVM 2.02.133 and e2fsprogs 1.42.13 tools successfully completed the LVM creation and online ext4 resize; package health remained clean.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","cmd-d9ea68e8daad47b9","cmd-2b402f6770ff43d4","cmd-2192380bdf204711","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The backing file and LVM creation, formatting and mounting, online growth, and persistence configuration directly supported the requested storage outcome and its recovery after the required restart. The pre- and post-restart probe files were bounded final evidence mutations, not abandoned troubleshooting. The restart was acknowledged, changed the boot identity, and recovered the service, mount, LV, preserved data, and read/write behavior. Lifecycle checks show no new failed services, listening sockets, package damage, or unrelated temporary residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-02f64ca6f129430c","cmd-133e5533403f4c2c","cmd-2b402f6770ff43d4","cmd-55e0cfe2c7e04757","cmd-f73fac42cbe540cd","cmd-f24031456d804a6e","cmd-2192380bdf204711","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct evidence. The LV was mounted, expanded online from 1 GiB to 2 GiB without losing its sentinel content, and recovered successfully after a genuine node restart. The operating-system release and kernel were unchanged. The provider-wide restart protocol for node1 is complete and internally consistent: healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, different post-restart boot identity, and recovered service, storage, data, and write behavior.","limitations":["The captured filesystem check before restart reported a free-inode-count discrepancy while e2fsck was run read-only against the mounted filesystem; it made no repair. Subsequent restart recovery and read/write probes were healthy, but no post-restart offline filesystem check was captured."]}
